\begin{abstract}
Exact model counting (\#SAT) is a fundamental problem in automated reasoning 
with applications in verification, diagnosis, and probabilistic inference. 
Although sequential model counters have become highly effective, 
many instances remain too expensive to solve on a single machine. 
This report presents a distributed framework for exact model counting based 
on a master-worker architecture. The framework partitions the search space 
into disjoint cubes, assigns them to workers, and aggregates the resulting counts. 
To reduce load imbalance caused by heterogeneous subproblem difficulty, 
it combines adaptive timeout estimation with dynamic cube resplitting. 
The implementation is solver-agnostic and uses gRPC for communication between components. 
We evaluate the framework on standard benchmark instances and discuss its limitations and possible extensions.
\end{abstract}

