\section{Conclusion}
\vspace{-1.5em}
Our framework addresses the scalability limitations of sequential model counting through a modular, 
gRPC-based distributed framework designed for heterogeneous environments. 
By decoupling the coordination layer from the underlying sequential engines, 
the framework maintains a solver-agnostic architecture that can integrate any DIMACS-compatible tool, 
such as Ganak2 or d4. The primary contribution of this work lies in the implementation of adaptive mechanisms, 
specifically dynamic cube resplitting and a self-calibrating timeout strategy, 
to mitigate the performance bottlenecks caused by heavy-tailed distributions of subproblem difficulty. 
Preliminary results indicate that while the framework achieves significant speedups.
\vspace{-1.5em}
\section{Limitations and improvements}
\vspace{-1.5em}
Preliminary results indicate that the framework can reduce overall solving time 
on instances with uneven subproblem difficulty, although its effectiveness 
depends on the quality of the initial decomposition and the variability of the backend solver.

A primary area of improvement lies in the cube decomposition process, 
where the integration of structural heuristics such as tree decomposition or VSIDS-based variable 
selection could facilitate the generation of more balanced and higher-quality subproblems. 

Furthermore, while the current dynamic timeout mechanism relies on a 
simple average of historical execution times, a more robust probabilistic 
model could be developed to estimate ${T_\text{limit}}$ based on the structural similarities between active and previously solved cubes.

To further optimize resource allocation, a "scouting" mechanism could be 
implemented where a subset of workers utilizes fast SAT heuristics 
to gauge the complexity of subproblems that appear stagnant. 
This approach would allow the system to intelligently distinguish between 
truly intractable sub-cubes and those nearing completion, enabling 
more informed decisions on whether to trigger a redistribution or grant a "mercy" extension to the current worker.

Beyond heuristic refinements, long-term research will explore the 
integration of distributed hash tables or databases to facilitate component cache sharing across the network. 
This would require a more deep integration with the internal workings of the underlying solvers,
but it has the potential to significantly reduce redundant computations by allowing workers to benefit from the progress of their peers.

Additionally, a portfolio-based approach could be implemented to deploy multiple solver types simultaneously,
allowing the framework to automatically select the most efficient backend for a given sub-cube.
